[FINAL-TEXT] ## Phase 3 — Confirming Evidence: Nail in the Coffin

The full rulesets reveal the exact mechanism:

**`dswa1` FORWARD chain:**
```
iifname "br0.10" meta l4proto icmp ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24  counter packets 6 bytes 504  accept
iifname "br0.10"               ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24  counter packets 0 bytes 0    drop
```

**`dswa2` FORWARD chain (identical structure):**
```
iifname "br0.10" meta l4proto icmp ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24  counter packets 0 bytes 0  accept
iifname "br0.10"               ip saddr 10.1.0.0/24 ip daddr 10.3.0.0/24  counter packets 0 bytes 0  drop
```

This is a **two-rule chain that explicitly permits only ICMP and drops everything else** (TCP, UDP, all other protocols) from Office A (10.1.0.0/24) toward Office B (10.3.0.0/24) when traffic arrives on the VLAN-10 bridge interface (`br0.10`):

| Traffic type | Hits which rule | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| ICMP ping (pc1→pc3) | Rule 1 (ICMP accept) | ✅ **passes** — explains why `get_reachability` showed 0% loss |
| TCP/UDP services (HTTP, etc.) | Falls through to Rule 2 (generic drop) | ❌ **silently dropped** — explains why services "hang" |

`dswa1`'s ICMP-accept counter shows **6 packets / 504 bytes** (confirming it is on the active path for PC1→Office-B traffic from the reachability test), while the DROP counter remains 0 (no service traffic has been attempted yet, consistent with only ICMP probes run).

**Direct evidence matches the ACL fault-family fingerprint.** Owning devices: **dswa1** and **dswa2** (the dual distribution switches for Office A, both carry identical injected rules). Advancing to Phase 5.

